  <section aria-labelledby="pattern-heading">
    <h2 id="pattern-heading">Patent + OSS pattern</h2>
    <p>
      The hybrid OSS-plus-patent posture is not unique to this project.
      It is the same posture used by <strong>Deque Systems</strong> for
      the axe-core accessibility engine
      (<a href="https://www.mozilla.org/en-US/MPL/2.0/" rel="external">MPL-2.0</a>,
      which carries an explicit patent grant under &sect;2.1) alongside
      Deque's accessibility-related patent portfolio: the full
      axe-core engine ships as OSS, and Deque monetises on operations
      (axe DevTools Pro, the
      <abbr title="Intelligent Guided Test">IGT</abbr> library,
      enterprise-tier services) rather than on closing the engine.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same posture appears across mainstream open-core leaders:
      GitLab Inc. (MIT Community Edition + patented enterprise
      extensions), Sentry (Functional Source License since 2024 +
      earlier <abbr title="Business Source License">BSL</abbr> with
      patent grants), PostHog (MIT + Apache-2.0 modules), and
      Mattermost (MIT client SDKs + AGPL-3.0 Team Edition). In each
      case the license-grant patent-peace clause does the work:
      <strong>OSS users get a guaranteed-irrevocable patent license
      scoped to use of the OSS code</strong>; the maintainer's
      portfolio remains enforceable against forks or rebuilds that
      step outside the licensed work.
    </p>

    <h3>EUPL-1.2 Article 2 &mdash; the narrow patent grant</h3>
    <p>
      EUPL-1.2 carries a narrower patent grant than Apache-2.0 or
      MPL-2.0. Article 2 final paragraph grants the Licensee
      &laquo;royalty-free, non-exclusive usage rights to any patents
      held by the Licensor, to the extent necessary to make use of the
      rights granted on the Work under this Licence&raquo;.
      Three properties of this grant matter:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Patent peace for OSS users.</strong> Anyone using
        EUPL-1.2-licensed code receives an irrevocable royalty-free
        patent license covering the maintainer's patents <em>to the
        extent necessary</em> to exercise the EUPL rights on the
        released Work. A developer running the OSS pipeline in their
        own CI faces zero patent-litigation risk from the maintainer's
        portfolio for that use. A community fork that stays within the
        licensed scope inherits the same peace.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Moat preservation against non-OSS commercial competitors.</strong>
        The &laquo;to the extent necessary&raquo; language is
        materially narrower than Apache-2.0 &sect;3's enumerated
        &laquo;make, use, sell, offer to sell, have made, import&raquo;
        grant. A commercial competitor cannot fork the OSS surface and
        use the fork as a back-door license to commercialise techniques
        covered by the closed core.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>EU sovereignty optics + CRA alignment.</strong>
        EUPL-1.2 is published in twenty-three EU official languages
        with each version legally equivalent, accepted by national
        public-sector procurement frameworks, and maps cleanly onto
        the Cyber Resilience Act Article 24 Open Source Steward
        documentation expectations.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The full reasoning, including comparison against MPL-2.0,
      Apache-2.0, AGPL-3.0, and the Fair Source family, lives in an
      internal license-choice memo summarised here; a public
      write-up will be linked from the repository once it lands in
      <code>docs/</code>.
    </p>
